CLAUDE.md lists PGP, AWS KMS, GCP KMS, Azure Key Vault and HashiCorp Vault
as later phases. File::SOPS::Backend::Age is still the only one. Split out
of karr #4.

SCOPE: HashiCorp Vault is the only realistic candidate for an actual
implementation. PGP, AWS KMS, GCP KMS and Azure KV are out of scope and
likely stay out — the metadata round-trip already covers reading documents
shared with such recipients, and "not implemented, refuses loudly" is the
defensible permanent answer for the cloud backends.

WHAT ALREADY EXISTS, and it is more than the title suggests:

- File::SOPS::Metadata models `pgp`, `kms`, `gcp_kms`, `azure_kv` and `hc_vault`
  as first-class attributes; `key_groups` and anything unrecognised survive in
  `extra`. A document carrying any of them round-trips through parse/serialize
  with those entries byte-identical (t/15-rotate.t pins it).
- key_material_fields() already answers "which backends does this document carry
  a wrapped data key for", and _assert_rekeyable() already refuses to rotate or
  edit such a document rather than silently revoking those recipients.
- A document shared with, say, PGP AND age recipients can already be READ here
  through its age entry, MAC and all. Only writing for the other backend is
  missing.

So the shape of the remaining work per backend is narrow and identical:

    encrypt_data_key(data_key => $bytes, recipients => \@ids) -> [ { ... } ]
    decrypt_data_key(<field> => \@entries, identities => ...) -> $bytes

plus the per-entry field names that backend writes into its section (vault:
`path`, `key`, `method`, `context`, `enc`, `created_at` — MEASURE them against
the sops binary, do not take them from the docs), plus dropping the backend
out of the _assert_rekeyable refusal list once it can re-wrap, plus a
`recipients` argument shape that can name which backend a recipient belongs to.

Order by cost, cheapest first:
1. HashiCorp Vault via HTTP against the transit engine — a token, a path, and
   a key name. Testable offline against a vault dev server in CI. This is the
   one to land when the use case arrives.
2. (out of scope) PGP via gpg CLI. The most likely "shared with an age file"
   candidate but no current need.
3. (out of scope, likely permanent) AWS/GCP/Azure KMS: each needs an SDK or
   hand-rolled request signing plus credentials, and none can be tested here
   without an account.

Whatever lands first must NOT weaken the guard in _assert_rekeyable for the
backends still missing.
